{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-66007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=11\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-10$\n- $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-66007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-66007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "704e5a2af5144cdecf45bfdb1f42009c34acf671b2dc64252522c3016c882065"} {"question" "q-66007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-66007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-66007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=12$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{7}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations I and III have no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "704e5a2af5144cdecf45bfdb1f42009c34acf671b2dc64252522c3016c882065"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-77809" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8}=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8} & =5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{8} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={45}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x-{360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/504427bf4d324547e1a8b142a2fce58818b32491269ce676533672a0762903b1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={135}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={135}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={-315}^{∘},\\,x={-225}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "704e5a2af5144cdecf45bfdb1f42009c34acf671b2dc64252522c3016c882065"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-315"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-275008" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-225"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation  $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 6\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})=(48.590\\,377…)^{∘}≈{48.590}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${48.590}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={48.590}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/b6754300e279bf09409a49aeca7160a319ee90b5a120fe7ac775f12a8444b8af.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{48.590}^{∘} \\\\  & ={131.410}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{49}^{∘},\\,x≈{131}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "704e5a2af5144cdecf45bfdb1f42009c34acf671b2dc64252522c3016c882065"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "49"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338750" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "131"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{5})=-0.643\\,501…≈-0.6435\n$$\n\nSince $-0.6435$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.6435.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/d6fbe829a100e0ee59872f9bc8d2d6501e88b389ada63008fbe1ef6e6eee7ee4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.6435 \\\\  & ≈3.7851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=3.7851$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=3.7851-2\\pi ≈-2.4981\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-2.5,\\,x=-0.6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d842f4f5bc4fbb080f412f6b4f6e06f6c9a6e3e7864d85f"} {"question" "q-338793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "704e5a2af5144cdecf45bfdb1f42009c34acf671b2dc64252522c3016c882065"} {"question" "q-338793" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/state.json" "file_sha256" "a0eef812c3455a177d